---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/ui/PageHeader.astro';
---

<Base title="About" page="about" lang="en" alternatePath="/about/">
  <div class="page-stack">
    <PageHeader eyebrow="About" title="About" lede="Identity, philosophy, and direction." />
    <section class="article-section">
      <h2>Identity</h2>
      <p>
        I am a Computer Science and Engineering student focused on optimization-based AI systems.
        More than building applications, I care about what happens beneath them — how data is stored
        and represented, and what objectives and feedback loops it moves along. At the end of that
        interest sits a single goal: designing AI models that are customized to an individual user
        and adapt dynamically as they are used.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="article-section">
      <h2>Philosophy of Computing</h2>
      <blockquote class="quote-panel">
        Computer science is the work of reading the flow of data, and building a city on the land
        called memory — a city with the infrastructure that lets data travel safely, correctly, and
        efficiently.
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        Every computation is, in the end, data flowing across memory. So I read a program as a city.
        Memory is the land the city stands on, data structures are the buildings where data resides,
        and functions and APIs are the roads connecting them. As with any good city, the conditions
        of a good system come down to three things: data must not be corrupted or intruded upon
        (safety), it must arrive where intended in the intended form (correctness), and it must move
        without unnecessary cost (efficiency).
      </p>
      <p>
        Seen this way, programming is less about erecting individual buildings than about designing
        infrastructure. Only when I can trace where data is created, where it flows, and where its
        cost accrues can I claim to understand a system — let alone optimize it.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="article-section">
      <h2>From Philosophy to Bi-level Optimization</h2>
      <p>
        A city is not finished the moment it is built. Once residents move in, their patterns of
        movement change, and the planner lays down new roads around those flows. This double
        structure — design reshaping adaptation, adaptation reshaping design — is exactly the
        structure of bi-level optimization, the field I want to study.
      </p>
      <p>
        At the upper level, a designer decides the environment: a game designer sets rules and
        difficulty; in NAS, an architecture is chosen. At the lower level, players find strategies
        and weights find optima within that environment. Upper-level decisions change lower-level
        adaptation, and that adaptation changes the next upper-level decision in turn. To me,
        bi-level optimization is my city-building philosophy translated into the language of
        learning systems.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="article-section">
      <h2>Final Goal: AI That Adapts to a Person</h2>
      <p>
        What I ultimately want to build is a city that redesigns itself for a single resident — an
        AI model customized to an individual user and dynamically adapting to them. Dynamic
        difficulty adjustment (DDA) is my first testing ground for this goal: a system that models a
        player and redesigns the environment around them is a miniature of personalized, adaptive
        AI.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
